  "tagline": "Rakuten's decentralised, memcached-compatible distributed key-value store written in Ruby; dormant since 2019.",
  "description": "ROMA is a distributed key-value store that Rakuten built and open-sourced for its own services. It is a pure peer-to-peer system shaped like a distributed hash table: every node is equal, routing tables map key ranges to nodes, and data is replicated so the cluster survives node loss and can add capacity without a coordinator. It is written in Ruby, speaks the memcached text protocol so any memcached client works, and ships Ruby and Java clients plus plugins and pluggable storage back-ends. The last release, 1.3.0, came out in January 2016, the final commit landed in 2019 and the roma-kvs.org site has gone offline.",
